# Chapter 5 — The Tick

Everything so far has happened *once*. You typed a thing, it drew, it stopped.
A game is different: a game is a thing that keeps happening. Mario falls a
little more each instant; the ghost creeps one step closer; the timer counts
down. What all of those share is a heartbeat — a moment that arrives, over and
over, and each time it arrives the world moves forward a notch.

That heartbeat is called the **tick**, and this is the chapter where the
console stops being a picture and starts being alive.

## The world has a frame clock

Motoi keeps a world with a clock in it. The clock does not run on its own at
the prompt — you advance it, one frame at a time, with `world/step`. Each step
is one heartbeat. `world/frame` tells you what beat you are on.

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(display (world/frame))
(newline)
```

```
3
```

Three steps, three frames — the clock reads `3`. That is the whole engine of
motion, hiding in plain sight. In a *running* game the console calls
`world/step` for you about sixty times a second, so the clock races ahead and
the world appears to move smoothly. At the prompt you get to be the clock
yourself, stepping one frame at a time so you can watch exactly what changes.

## Things with velocity move on every step

A frame clock is only interesting if something changes when it ticks. Put a
thing in the world, give it a **velocity** — a speed and direction — and every
`world/step` slides it along:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define ball (world/spawn 'ball 10 20))
(entity/set-vel! ball 3 0)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(display (entity/x ball))
(newline)
```

```
16
```

You turned gravity off (`world/gravity! 0 0`) so nothing else would pull on the
ball, spawned a `ball` at `x = 10`, and gave it a velocity of `3` to the right.
Two steps later its `x` is `16` — it moved `3` on the first tick and `3` on the
second. **Velocity plus the tick is motion.** You never wrote a loop that
nudged the ball; you set a speed and let the heartbeat do the nudging. That is
the shift this chapter is really teaching: you describe *how a thing changes per
frame*, and the tick applies that change forever.

## Gravity makes the change change

Constant velocity is a straight line. Real movement usually speeds up or slows
down — and that is what gravity is: a pull that adds a little speed every tick.
Motoi lets each thing decide how much gravity touches it, with
`entity/gravity-scale!`.

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 1)
(define drop (world/spawn 'drop 60 0))
(entity/gravity-scale! drop 1)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(world/step)
(display (entity/y drop))
(newline)
```

```
6
```

Watch the numbers hiding behind that `6`. Gravity adds `1` to the drop's
downward speed each tick. After tick one it has fallen `1` (total `1`). After
tick two its speed is `2`, so it falls to `3`. After tick three its speed is
`3`, so it lands at `6`. **1, 3, 6** — the gap grows every frame. That is why a
falling thing looks like it accelerates: because it does. You just watched a
real fall happen three frames at a time.

Setting the scale per-thing is a gift: a balloon can have gravity-scale `0` and
drift, a boulder can have `1` and plummet, and both live in the same world.

## Schedule something for later

Not everything happens every frame. Sometimes you want a thing to happen *once,
soon* — a door opens after a beat, an egg hatches after a delay. `world/after`
takes a number of frames and a thing to do, and runs it when that many ticks
have gone by:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(define hatched #f)
(world/after 3 (lambda () (set! hatched #t)))
(world/step)
(world/step)
(display hatched)
(newline)
(world/step)
(display hatched)
(newline)
```

```
#f
#t
```

You scheduled a hatching for three frames out. After two steps it had not
happened yet — `hatched` was still `#f`. The third step reached the deadline,
the console ran your lambda, and `hatched` flipped to `#t`. This is your first
taste of *timed* logic: instead of checking the clock yourself every frame, you
hand the console a deadline and a job, and it fires the job at the right tick.

## What you just proved

You found the console's heartbeat and learned to advance it by hand. You gave a
thing a velocity and watched the tick carry it. You turned on gravity and
watched a fall accelerate, frame by frame, `1 3 6`. And you scheduled a job for
a future tick and saw it fire exactly on time. Nothing here looped, and nothing
here was magic — every motion was a small change applied once per heartbeat.

You can now move things on their own. What you cannot do yet is *push* them —
the world still ignores you. Next chapter wires up the buttons, and the moment a
button can change what happens on the next tick, you are holding a game
controller.

> **Tip**
>
> When you are debugging motion, step the world **one frame at a time** and
> print the value you care about between steps, exactly like the falls above.
> Stepping by hand is the single best way to see what your per-frame change is
> really doing — the live 60-per-second loop is too fast to read.

> **Warning**
>
> A thing only feels gravity if its `gravity-scale` is above zero. If you spawn
> something and it stubbornly refuses to fall, you almost certainly forgot
> `(entity/gravity-scale! thing 1)`. This is a feature, not a bug — it is how
> Motoi lets floating and falling things share one world — but it surprises
> everyone once. Appendix D lays out the tick and the scheduler in full.

